1
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:07,981
On the fifth of November, every breakout room in the PM Summit session

2
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,933
“PM Role in an AI World” will work on a real project problem with AI.

3
00:00:13,333 --> 00:00:18,662
This briefing explains the situation, what to prepare, and what your room will deliver.

4
00:00:19,562 --> 00:00:23,346
Meet KESTREL: a new sensor-hub chip and its evaluation kit,

5
00:00:23,366 --> 00:00:27,821
two weeks from tape-out, with a lead customer waiting for samples.

6
00:00:28,121 --> 00:00:30,976
The project is fictional. The pressure is not.

7
00:00:31,576 --> 00:00:35,226
On Monday, you inherit KESTREL as its new project manager.

8
00:00:35,246 --> 00:00:39,115
By Thursday morning, you are holding six problems at once.

9
00:00:39,365 --> 00:00:42,827
A customer change request that nobody can answer yet.

10
00:00:42,927 --> 00:00:45,922
A risk log that no one has touched in five weeks.

11
00:00:46,022 --> 00:00:49,144
A meeting where nobody wrote down what was decided.

12
00:00:49,244 --> 00:00:52,155
A status report that may no longer be green.

13
00:00:52,255 --> 00:00:56,657
A steering decision due at two o’clock, with five conflicting opinions.

14
00:00:56,757 --> 00:00:59,688
And lessons learned that arrived three days too late.

15
00:01:00,188 --> 00:01:03,907
None of this is unusual. It is the week most of us know.

16
00:01:04,057 --> 00:01:09,525
And none of it is the part of the job that matters most: leading a team to good decisions.

17
00:01:10,525 --> 00:01:15,368
Before the summit, we ask for about twenty minutes of preparation, in four steps.

18
00:01:15,718 --> 00:01:20,110
One. Read “Week 38”, the KESTREL story attached to your invitation.

19
00:01:20,130 --> 00:01:22,305
It takes about twelve minutes.

20
00:01:22,605 --> 00:01:26,398
Two. Open your room’s case file at the back of the story.

21
00:01:26,418 --> 00:01:32,571
Your invitation tells you which one, from C1 to C6. Read it as if the problem were yours.

22
00:01:32,871 --> 00:01:34,811
Three. Find what doesn’t add up.

23
00:01:34,831 --> 00:01:39,712
Every case file hides at least two things a careful project manager would catch.

24
00:01:39,732 --> 00:01:43,301
Note them, and keep them to yourself until the session.

25
00:01:43,601 --> 00:01:48,248
Four. Bring one real moment from your own work when this problem cost you time,

26
00:01:48,268 --> 00:01:50,418
and a rough estimate of how much.

27
00:01:50,918 --> 00:01:53,062
You don’t need any AI experience.

28
00:01:53,082 --> 00:01:57,740
Rooms mix beginners and experts on purpose, and everyone has a role.

29
00:01:57,890 --> 00:02:02,567
Please don’t bring confidential documents. All the data we use is fictional.

30
00:02:03,567 --> 00:02:06,601
On the day, each room takes one of the six moments.

31
00:02:06,701 --> 00:02:09,723
In sixty minutes, you design an AI assistant for it,

32
00:02:09,743 --> 00:02:14,097
test it on the case file, and check whether it catches what you caught.

33
00:02:14,447 --> 00:02:20,382
Your room leaves with a Pilot Pack: the assistant’s instructions, the decision the PM keeps,

34
00:02:20,402 --> 00:02:26,816
one metric, one guardrail, an owner, and a first test to run on a real project after the summit.

35
00:02:27,716 --> 00:02:31,665
The assistant takes the routine. Every decision stays yours.

36
00:02:32,265 --> 00:02:36,672
Read “Week 38” before the fifth of November. We’ll see you in the room.
